\section{What a language does not change}{


Only DISPLAY strings translate. The column names of the exported
frames (\code{as.data.frame()}, \code{tidy()}, \code{as_structured()}) are a
documented contract that user code indexes into, so \code{out[["Yes \%"]]}
resolves under every language, and so do the block identities, the
encoded cell tokens and the mathematical glyphs. Errors, warnings and
messages stay English: they are read by developers and quoted in bug
reports.

One exception, and it follows from the same rule. A column named
after a LEVEL of \code{by} takes that level's own spelling -- which is why
\code{"Yes \%"} stays \code{"Yes \%"}: your data is never translated. spicy's own
missing category is such a level, so it is the one name that does
move: \code{table_categorical(by = )} on a variable with missing values
gives \code{"(Missing) n"} in English and \code{"(Manquant) n"} under \code{"fr"}
(or whatever \code{row_missing_level} is overridden to). Address that
column through \code{row_missing_level} rather than by typing it.
}

\section{Numbers follow the language too}{


A language brings its typographic locale with it, so
\code{options(spicy.language = "fr")} is one gesture for a coherent
French table: French words, comma decimal mark, and the leading zero
French typography keeps on a p-value (\verb{0,003}). The sources are the
BIPM's SI brochure and the European Union's \emph{Code de redaction
interinstitutionnel}; \code{\link[=spicy_style]{spicy_style()}} quotes them. \code{"en"} brings no
locale, and nothing changes for anyone who sets no language.

The locale sits at the BOTTOM of the formatting resolution. A
journal style outranks it -- \code{style = "jama"} under \code{"fr"} gives
JAMA's p-values and the French comma, \code{style = "lancet"} keeps the
journal's midline decimal point -- and an argument you type outranks
both, so \code{decimal_mark = "."} gives French words with a decimal
point.
